"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,362\nWhy neuromyths stick\n\n4\n00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:13,318\nHere is a puzzle.\n\n5\n00:00:13,707 --> 00:00:18,410\nTeachers who know more about the brain are not less likely to believe brain myths.\n\n6\n00:00:18,724 --> 00:00:20,845\nIn one study, they were more likely.\n\n7\n00:00:21,015 --> 00:00:28,812\nIn 2002, an O E C D project on brain and learning gave these ideas a name: neuromyths.\n\n8\n00:00:29,170 --> 00:00:31,752\nMisreadings of real brain research,\n\n9\n00:00:31,930 --> 00:00:34,032\nstretched into claims about teaching.\n\n10\n00:00:34,202 --> 00:00:41,313\nIn 2012, Sanne Dekker and colleagues surveyed two hundred forty two teachers in England and the Netherlands,\n\n11\n00:00:41,605 --> 00:00:43,323\nall interested in the brain.\n\n12\n00:00:43,964 --> 00:00:47,531\nOn average, they believed forty nine percent of the myths.\n\n13\n00:00:47,825 --> 00:00:51,844\nAnd teachers with more general brain knowledge believed more myths,\n\n14\n00:00:52,014 --> 00:00:52,959\nnot fewer.\n\n15\n00:00:53,129 --> 00:00:57,558\nA larger American study in 2017 compared groups.\n\n16\n00:00:57,998 --> 00:01:01,355\nThe general public endorsed sixty eight percent of the myths,\n\n17\n00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:03,725\neducators fifty six percent,\n\n18\n00:01:03,979 --> 00:01:06,935\nand people with high exposure to neuroscience,\n\n19\n00:01:07,155 --> 00:01:08,454\nforty six percent.\n\n20\n00:01:08,968 --> 00:01:11,669\nTraining helped, but did not eliminate them.\n\n21\n00:01:11,839 --> 00:01:12,927\nWhy do myths stick?\n\n22\n00:01:13,355 --> 00:01:15,373\nMost start from a real finding.\n\n23\n00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:18,719\nThey offer a simple story and an easy fix.\n\n24\n00:01:18,999 --> 00:01:21,316\nThey are sold in training and products.\n\n25\n00:01:21,577 --> 00:01:24,685\nAnd brain language makes a claim feel scientific.\n\n26\n00:01:24,855 --> 00:01:27,624\nThis series takes twelve popular ideas,\n\n27\n00:01:27,725 --> 00:01:28,810\none at a time.\n\n28\n00:01:29,287 --> 00:01:30,531\nWhere each came from,\n\n29\n00:01:30,551 --> 00:01:32,130\nwhat the evidence shows,\n\n30\n00:01:32,247 --> 00:01:34,247\nthe grain of truth if there is one,\n\n31\n00:01:34,436 --> 00:01:35,923\nand what to do instead.\n\n32\n00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:37,199\nDo this instead.\n\n33\n00:01:37,698 --> 00:01:39,885\nWhen a teaching claim mentions the brain,\n\n34\n00:01:39,902 --> 00:01:41,490\nask what was actually tested,\n\n35\n00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:42,782\nand on what outcome.\n\n36\n00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:44,723\nLook for the original study,\n\n37\n00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:45,737\nnot the summary.\n\n38\n00:01:46,008 --> 00:01:49,084\nAnd be extra careful with ideas that sell a product.\n\n39\n00:01:49,715 --> 00:01:53,732\nOne tip to share with students: when a claim mentions the brain,\n\n40\n00:01:53,752 --> 00:01:54,980\nask how they know.\n\n41\n00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:57,310\nInterest in the brain is a good thing.\n\n42\n00:01:57,626 --> 00:02:00,570\nIt just needs a partner: a habit of checking.\n\n"